---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'Overtime FAQ | overtimechecker.com';
const description = 'Common questions about overtime pay: salaried workers, off-the-clock work, comp time, double-time, and more.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Overtime FAQ</h1>

  <h2>I'm salaried — am I exempt from overtime?</h2>
  <p>Not automatically. Exemption requires both a salary at or above the federal threshold AND meeting the duties test for executive, administrative, or professional work. Being called a "manager" or "supervisor" doesn't matter &mdash; what you actually do does.</p>

  <h2>Does my employer have to pay me time-and-a-half on weekends?</h2>
  <p>Federally, no. Overtime is based on hours worked over 40 in a workweek, not on which day. Some union contracts or state-specific industries require weekend premiums; check your contract or state rules.</p>

  <h2>Can my employer give me "comp time" instead of overtime pay?</h2>
  <p>For private-sector employees: no. Public-sector employees can sometimes receive comp time at 1.5 hours per overtime hour worked, but with restrictions.</p>

  <h2>What about double-time?</h2>
  <p>Federal FLSA doesn't require double-time. Some states (California most notably) require double-time after specific hour thresholds. Check our <a href="/state-laws/">State Laws</a> page.</p>

  <h2>Does off-the-clock work count?</h2>
  <p>Yes. Work you perform before clocking in, after clocking out, during unpaid meal breaks, or remotely (taking work calls, answering email at home) generally counts toward your hours.</p>

  <h2>What if I worked through lunch?</h2>
  <p>If your employer required or allowed you to work through your meal break, that time is compensable and counts toward overtime.</p>

  <h2>How long do I have to file a wage claim?</h2>
  <p>Federal: 2 years (3 if willful). State limits vary; many states allow longer. Don't delay.</p>

  <h2>Will I get fired if I complain about unpaid overtime?</h2>
  <p>Retaliation is illegal under FLSA and most state laws. If you're fired or punished for asserting wage rights, that's a separate violation with its own remedies.</p>
</BaseLayout>
